Count small saturated carbon skeletons without counting drawings twice (@chemistry-skeleton-counts: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Separate-a-molecular-conformation-from-a-drawing-and-a-physical-pathway.lingua#r_00A70A2D6280013B3B40B03DDC") { r_00E0A2FD34574493511BF8F2AC
State a narrow model before enumerating structures: connected acyclic carbon skeletons, carbon degree at most four, ordinary single bonds, neutral valence completion with hydrogen, and no isotope labels. Each carbon vertex of degree d receives 4−d hydrogens. A tree on n vertices has n−1 edges; prove this by removing a leaf and applying induction, starting with one vertex and zero edges. The degree sum counts each edge twice, giving Σd=2(n−1). Hence total hydrogen count is Σ(4−d)=4n−2(n−1)=2n+2.

This derivation explains the acyclic saturated formula C_nH_(2n+2). It does not apply unchanged to a ring, multiple bond, charge, radical or different valence model. A ring consumes an extra pair of valences in the analogous single-bond account, but general chemical existence still needs evidence beyond the count. In particular, the tree program rejects degree-five carbon and does not model electron-deficient or multicenter bonding.

For n=4 there are only two unrooted tree shapes: the path and the three-leaf star. If no degree exceeds two, connectedness forces a path; otherwise the maximum possible degree is three and uses all other vertices as neighbors. These correspond to the two constitutional C₄H₁₀ structures. For n=5, maximum degree two again gives a path; maximum degree four gives the four-leaf star; and maximum degree three leaves exactly one degree-two vertex extending one of its three arms. Thus there are three shapes. This is an exhaustiveness argument, not merely three successful drawings.

The bounded published checkpoint is [Organic Chemistry tenth edition by John McMurry](../sources/Organic-Chemistry-tenth-edition-by-John-McMurry.lingua#r_00F02946DB1C7E37631D69129A), section 3.2 and its Table 3.2. Its counts through C₆H₁₄ agree with the independent enumeration: for n=1,…,6 the numbers of constitutional skeleton classes are 1,1,1,2,3,5. The enumerator first considers every n−1-edge subset of all possible vertex pairs, rejects disconnected or over-valent candidates, and groups accepted trees by their proved canonical code. This exhausts the declared finite model. It does not count conformers, isotopomers or stereoisomers separately.

Keeping arbitrary vertex numbers instead gives 1,1,3,16,125,1290 accepted labeled trees over the same n range. Dividing by n! would be wrong because different shapes have different symmetries. For a fixed tree T, let Aut(T) be its label permutations that preserve its edge set. There are n! label permutations; two yield the same labeled image exactly when their relative permutation is an automorphism. Every resulting image therefore has |Aut(T)| preimages, and its orbit contains n!/|Aut(T)| distinct labeled trees. At n=4 the path has two automorphisms and twelve labelings, while the star has six automorphisms and four labelings. Their sum is sixteen, not two times a common labeling count.

An independent enumeration uses a leaf-removal code. Repeatedly remove the smallest numbered leaf and record its neighbor until two vertices remain. The code has n−2 entries. To decode, give vertex i degree one plus its number of remaining occurrences, connect the smallest degree-one vertex to the next code entry, decrement both degrees and finish with the final pair. The encoding and decoding undo one another at each step, proving the correspondence for labeled trees when n≥2. The degree formula permits rejection of degree above four independently of the edge-subset search. For n=6 the six labeled degree-five stars are excluded from 6⁴=1296 possible tree codes, leaving 1290.

**Work:** prove the n=5 classification and verify both n=4 orbit sizes. Identify the two n=6 classes with the same degree sequence and show why the stronger tree code distinguishes them. Compare the published small counts with the program while preserving the scope of the reconstruction. Larger enumeration, full nomenclature, cyclic structures and organic chemical behavior remain later studies, not consequences of these counts.

